      "writtenOn": "2025-10-30T14:11:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The reason I thought it would be useful is because we own the VM code as well and it would be on us to fix the VMs if this happens. So, having an error in dmesg would be easier to identify the cause of VM crash and then fix it. As you mentioned error limiting would be good and it might not be worth when upstreaming as well.\n\nI\u0027m fine with leaving it as it is, if you feel its not worth it. Resolving",
      "parentUuid": "85160783_52d11b81",
      "revId": "cc9f8394c1a312bb287f2381e6a306f35af9af9f",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1c8a9350_f6091665",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/vmx/vmx.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 4755,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-10-30T16:19:55Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "What I meant by \"I doubt it would be very useful anyway\" is that a mere notice that a pVM has executed a string IO instruction wouldn\u0027t tell us _why_ it executed it. It wouldn\u0027t even tell the address where it executed it (unless we also dump `kvm_rip_read(vcpu)` or something; but then, why just in this case?). And I suppose that in practice in virtually all cases it\u0027s gonna execute it accidentally, not intentionally. [*]\n\nMeanwhile, we inject #GP into the pVM here anyway, so generally the pVM has a chance to handle it, i.e. dump its stack etc...\n\n[*] At least with our Linux pVMs deliberate execution of string IO shouldn\u0027t happen, since we set CC_ATTR_GUEST_UNROLL_STRING_IO. As for the infamous non-Linux one - well, dunno. :)",
      "parentUuid": "f66b415c_db270a41",
